A||||7||Turbo1dr||Just got a 86 SE V6 Auto. Plenty of questions...||||1||
Z||000000||Turbo1dr||02-26-2001||10:29 PM||||The reason for getting this car is they are light and engine swaps choices that are available. The web sites I've visited so far on Fiero's are great! I have a 87 Buick Grand National Turbo/Intercooled 3.8L engine in my 78 Malibu. I done the complete swap which was basically a bolt in. Now my interest is to put this engine in my Fiero. Dropping almost 1000lbs in weight has <B>GOT</B> to be good!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Questions:<P>1. I'm thinking the easiest way is to get a FWD 86-87 block since they are the most related to the RWD. Will the flywheel where it bolts to the crank be able to withstand 600 HP? I know the bolt circle is smaller so that's why I'm concerned. I don't want to twist it off.<P>2. Since the SBC can be mated to the Fiero's 125 transmission by an adapter is there one available for a RWD Buick V6 engine?<P>3. I've read where the stock axles can stand a lot of HP but has any one gone mid 9's at 140+ in the 1/4 with them?<P>4. Who sells "beefy" 125 transmission parts?<P>5. Where can I get aftermarket performance catalogs for the Fiero?  I already get a SUMMIT catalog (aka..."The Chevy and Ford Parts Bible")<P>6. Do I need to forget about 600 HP in a Fiero and wake up from my dream?<P>TIA<P><BR>------------------<BR><B>78 Malibu</B> 3.8L Turbo Buick<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Homex.html" TARGET=_blank>TURBO WONDER</A><BR>Best 8th: 1.548, 6.62@106.32  3450lb<BR>TE-70 3 bolt, Ported Stock Iron Heads and Intake<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Modifications_files/Intercooler.jpg" TARGET=_blank>2.5 stock intercoolers</A><BR>Vernon Swindell, Jr.<p>[This message has been edited by Turbo1dr (edited 02-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||sqoach||02-26-2001||10:51 PM||||i don't know the answers to your fist five, but question #6 i would have to say yes. unless you're going pro-stock or something. the most hp i've seen in a street legal fiero was 450. and that was with a v8.<P>of course there could be others i haven't seen. i definately don't know it all when it comes to fieros, but i do know enough to get me by.<P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig2.JPG"> <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Turbo1dr||02-26-2001||11:08 PM||||That's why I posted #6 because I figured I would get a lot of "yes" answers on that one. The engine in my Malibu is VERY docile and has excellant street manners! It's driven almost everyday. It's getting to the point that I'm starting to "max" out on getting any more HP from a stock block, head, and intake engine. So I figured instead of chopping up my Malibu to loose weight, I'd rather put the engine in a lighter car.<P>------------------<BR><B>78 Malibu</B> 3.8L Turbo Buick<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Homex.html" TARGET=_blank>TURBO WONDER</A><BR>Best 8th: 1.548, 6.62@106.32  3450lb<BR>TE-70 3 bolt, Ported Stock Iron Heads and Intake<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Modifications_files/Intercooler.jpg" TARGET=_blank>2.5 stock intercoolers</A><BR>Vernon Swindell, Jr.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Hank is Here||02-26-2001||11:41 PM||||I am not 100% positive but I don't think that there is room for the turbo on the 3.8 in the Fiero engine compt, I could be worng though.<P>Why not upgrade the tranny too like a 440-T4 or something that came behind a Caddy FWD V8, at least they came behind more powerful engine than the 2.8   Also I believe the newer 4 speeds are quicker than the old 125||||reg||1
Z||000004||Turbo1dr||02-27-2001||12:13 AM||||I'm open to any suggestions. The 440-T4 is an option to use. <P>I'll have to relocate the turbo on the rear of the engine above the transmission. I've got a plan on how to get the turbo and intercooler hooked up. I don't know how reliable or how much HP the 125 and axles can actually take but if I get it in there I'll definately find out. I think the chain in the transmission will probably snap first.<P><BR>I have no idea who ownes this car but it has a 87 Buick GN engine.<P> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=852541&a=6201108&p=25250013">  <P> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=852541&a=6201108&p=25250011">  <P> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=852541&a=6201108&p=25250012">  <P>------------------<BR><B>78 Malibu</B> 3.8L Turbo Buick<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Homex.html" TARGET=_blank>TURBO WONDER</A><BR>Best 8th: 1.548, 6.62@106.32  3450lb<BR>TE-70 3 bolt, Ported Stock Iron Heads and Intake<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Modifications_files/Intercooler.jpg" TARGET=_blank>2.5 stock intercoolers</A><BR>Vernon Swindell, Jr.||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||02-27-2001||05:33 PM||||I know theres lots of 3.8 turbo Fieros around, so must be fairly ez. I stayed with 3.1 myself, added a turbo aftermarket kit. I think Archie says stock axles are good to 300 HP (V8 Archie) <p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 02-27-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||02-27-2001||06:24 PM||||I can't believe someone bothered. That's a bit much. I'd have been driving a Vette before I considered that piece of art. Trailer queen.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Turbo1dr||02-28-2001||12:19 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by terryk:<BR><B>I can't believe someone bothered. That's a bit much. I'd have been driving a Vette before I considered that piece of art. Trailer queen.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>terryk,<P>A bit much? Probably so for the Fiero. But I like to go fast. That's why I'm here asking these kinds of questions. 350 HP just isn't enough for me anymore...400 either. It's waaay too easy to get more than that from a Buick Turbo V6. My Malibu is every bit as drivable as a 14 second car.  Trailer Queen???  I hardly think driving to the track 70 miles round trip, getting over 23 MPG, and putting down mid to high 6's at 106 mph in the 8th is a trailer queen! In fact my car has NEVER been trailered and can quietly go through a Banks drive through window in the summer. Obviously you don't know about the power and drivability of a Buick Grand National Turbo V6. I posted this here to help me get some answers about high horsepower on Fiero's. I'm just here to learn. <P>------------------<BR><B>78 Malibu</B> 3.8L Turbo Buick<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Homex.html" TARGET=_blank>TURBO WONDER</A><BR>Best 8th: 1.548, 6.62@106.32  3450lb<BR>TE-70 3 bolt, Ported Stock Iron Heads and Intake<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/turbowonder/Malibu/Modifications_files/Intercooler.jpg" TARGET=_blank>2.5 stock intercoolers</A><BR>Vernon Swindell, Jr.||||reg||1
